**Convention 2.2 (source-independent fixed-observer comparison).** Let $S$ be one finite exact support containing 1 and 2. When two installed source priors are compared, keep the observer's very same binary code/data, initialization $\eta$, kernels $P_x$, complete reports $D_z$ and decoder consumers. It has no runtime prior input. For an actual word $h$, $\rho_h=\eta P_{h_1}\cdots P_{h_t}$ is therefore unchanged. Every legal finite word has positive likelihood at every depth; deterministic original records/events add no likelihood factors. The actual history domain is common to all positive priors on $S$. Both compatibility conditions concern those same rows, laws, histories and positive predicted prefixes, so their admissible fixed-code pool is independent of the prior. This is [EF] Theorems 7.2/8.2's fixed-observer comparison, not retraining or averaging different codes.

